# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files - never commit real values
.env*
# ...except the template. It holds only placeholders, and src/lib/env.ts points
# at it by name when a variable is missing, so it has to be in the repo.
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Local development database (PGlite)
.pglite/

# The pictures pulled off Instagram by `npm run instagram:archive`.
#
# Tens of megabytes of JPEGs and reels, none of which belong in a source
# repository - and the point of archiving them is to get them into Blob
# storage, not into git. The manifest beside them is committed, so the record
# of what was taken survives even where the files do not.
scripts/data/instagram/*
!scripts/data/instagram/manifest.json
!scripts/data/instagram/README.md
# Written by `npm run blob:upload` and read by `npm run db:import-events`, so
# an import needs neither the pictures nor a Blob token.
!scripts/data/instagram/uploads.json
# Written by `npm run media:upload` and read by the curated gallery and
# committee importers.
!scripts/data/instagram/media-uploads.json

# The jali reference photograph.
#
# The 17th-century screen it shows is long out of copyright, but the photograph
# of it is a separate work and we hold no licence for it - it carries the marks
# of a studio or catalogue shot. It was used as visual reference only; the
# lattice and arch on the site are original SVG (src/components/jali.tsx), so
# nothing depends on this file. Keep it out of the history: committing it is
# far easier than removing it later.
#
# The rest of attachments/ is the committee's own work and stays in the repo.
attachments/jali-new.jpg
